#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
issue_pipeline — the deterministic half of the fleet's three-tier issue pipeline.

The fleet already has a loop that FIXES BROKEN WORKFLOWS (fleet-pulse → doctor).
This is the loop that RESOLVES ISSUES, and it runs in three tiers:

  T1  INTAKE     every new open issue is enriched: gaps in the report are filled,
                 the body is rewritten into the house template, an isolated
                 virtual environment reproduces it and attaches real EVIDENCE
                 (install/lint/test/build logs, screenshots, candidate files),
                 then type / priority / size / owner labels are applied.
  T2  IMPLEMENT  an issue that reached `agent:ready` is implemented on a branch
                 and lands as a DRAFT pull request.
  T3  COMPLETE   that PR is driven to mergeable: CI green, tests and docs
                 present, body accurate — then marked ready for review.

This module owns everything DETERMINISTIC about that: which issues are at which
tier, what is missing from each, how urgent it is, how big it is, who owns it,
whether it may proceed autonomously, and how much work each tier may take on.
The AI tiers only ever act on a pre-vetted, capped, deduped queue — the same
guarantee that keeps the remediation loop from spamming the fleet.

STATE LIVES IN LABELS, NOT IN A DATABASE
----------------------------------------
Every stage transition is a GitHub label (`agent:ready`, `agent:in-pr`, …).
That choice is deliberate:

  * it survives a lost run, a cancelled job, and a rewritten data file — the
    pipeline is a SWEEP over current state, not a chain of events that dies
    silently when one link doesn't fire (the exact failure that froze this
    fleet's data for three weeks);
  * a human can re-queue, skip, or stop any issue by editing a label, with no
    tooling and no commit;
  * `agent:hold` and `human-review` are honoured everywhere, so opting out is
    always one click.

Hidden markers in comments/PR bodies are the audit trail and the second dedupe
guard; the labels are the state machine.

Auth: a token from GH_TOKEN / GITHUB_TOKEN, else `gh auth token`. Unreachable
repos are recorded, never fatal. Exit status is 0 with an empty queue — a fleet
with nothing to triage is the healthy steady state.
"""
